# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Ch 5 Unit 1 National income accounting
BANK['fnd-p4-ch05-u01'] = [
    Q('c9', 'An economy has private consumption ₹600 crore, investment ₹200 crore, government purchases ₹150 crore, exports ₹80 crore and imports ₹100 crore. GDP at market prices is:',
      ['₹930 crore', '₹1,130 crore', '₹1,030 crore', '₹950 crore'],
      'By the expenditure method, add consumption (600), investment (200) and government purchases (150), and then net exports (80 − 100 = −20): the total is ₹930 crore. Net exports are negative here because imports exceed exports. Adding imports instead of subtracting them gives the wrong figure of ₹1,130 crore.',
      'GDPmp = C + I + G + (X − M); imports are subtracted.', 40, 'core', '600+200+150+(80-100)'),
    Q('c5', 'GDP at market prices is ₹1,000 crore, depreciation ₹100 crore, net factor income from abroad −₹20 crore, indirect taxes ₹120 crore and subsidies ₹20 crore. National income (NNP at factor cost) is:',
      ['₹780 crore', '₹820 crore', '₹880 crore', '₹760 crore'],
      'NNP at factor cost = GDPmp − depreciation + NFIA − (indirect taxes − subsidies) = 1,000 − 100 + (−20) − (120 − 20) = ₹780 crore. Remember that net indirect taxes are deducted to move from market prices to factor cost, and a negative NFIA reduces the figure.',
      'NI = GDPmp − Dep + NFIA − NIT.', 50, 'moderate', '1000-100+(-20)-(120-20)'),
    Q('c7', 'A farmer grows wheat worth ₹100 (no purchased inputs), a miller turns it into flour sold for ₹160, and a baker sells bread made from it for ₹250. The contribution of this chain to GDP is:',
      ['₹250', '₹510', '₹410', '₹160'],
      'Value added at each stage: farmer ₹100, miller ₹160 − ₹100 = ₹60, baker ₹250 − ₹160 = ₹90. Total value added = ₹250, which equals the value of the final good, bread. Adding all sales (₹510) would count the wheat and flour more than once — the problem of double counting.',
      'Sum of value added = value of final goods; never add all sales.', 45, 'moderate', '100+(160-100)+(250-160)'),
    Q('c2', 'Nominal GDP is ₹1,200 lakh crore and real GDP (at base-year prices) is ₹1,000 lakh crore. The GDP deflator is:',
      ['120', '83.33', '20', '220'],
      'GDP deflator = (nominal GDP ÷ real GDP) × 100 = (1,200 ÷ 1,000) × 100 = 120. It shows that the general price level of all goods and services in GDP is 20% higher than in the base year. 83.33 inverts the ratio, and 20 is only the percentage rise.',
      'Deflator = nominal ÷ real × 100.', 30, 'core', '1200/1000*100'),
    Q('c2', 'Nominal GDP of a year is ₹1,500 crore and the GDP deflator is 125. Real GDP is:',
      ['₹1,200 crore', '₹1,875 crore', '₹1,375 crore', '₹1,250 crore'],
      'Real GDP = nominal GDP ÷ deflator × 100 = 1,500 ÷ 125 × 100 = ₹1,200 crore. Real GDP removes the effect of price changes, so it is lower than nominal GDP when prices have risen since the base year. Multiplying instead of dividing gives the wrong ₹1,875 crore.',
      'Real = nominal ÷ deflator × 100.', 30, 'core', '1500/125*100'),
    Q('c11', 'Which of these is included while estimating national income?',
      ['Imputed rent of owner-occupied houses', 'Old-age pension paid by the government', 'Scholarships given to students', 'Sale of a second-hand car'],
      'National income counts the value of current production of goods and services. Owner-occupied houses provide housing services, so an imputed rent is included. Pensions and scholarships are transfer payments with no current production in return, and sale of a used car is a transfer of an old asset, so all three are excluded.',
      'Included: current production (even imputed). Excluded: transfers, second-hand sales.', 30),
    Q('c11', 'A person buys shares worth ₹1,00,000 through a broker and pays a brokerage of ₹500. What is included in national income?',
      ['Only the brokerage of ₹500', 'The full ₹1,00,000', '₹1,00,500', 'Nothing'],
      'Buying shares is a financial transaction that only transfers ownership of existing claims; it creates no new goods or services, so ₹1,00,000 is excluded. The broker\'s commission, however, is payment for a current service and is included in national income.',
      'Financial transactions out; the service fee (brokerage) in.', 35, 'moderate'),
    Q('c8', 'Compensation of employees ₹500 crore, rent ₹60 crore, interest ₹40 crore, profit ₹150 crore and mixed income of the self-employed ₹100 crore. Net domestic product at factor cost (income method) is:',
      ['₹850 crore', '₹750 crore', '₹700 crore', '₹810 crore'],
      'By the income method, add wages and salaries (compensation of employees, 500), the operating surplus made up of rent, interest and profit (60 + 40 + 150), and the mixed income of the self-employed (100): NDP at factor cost is ₹850 crore. Leaving out mixed income would understate it at ₹750 crore.',
      'Income method = CoE + rent + interest + profit + mixed income.', 40, 'core', '500+60+40+150+100'),
    Q('c4', 'GVA at factor cost of an economy is ₹900 crore, production taxes are ₹30 crore and production subsidies ₹10 crore. GVA at basic prices is:',
      ['₹920 crore', '₹940 crore', '₹880 crore', '₹900 crore'],
      'GVA at basic prices = GVA at factor cost + production taxes − production subsidies = 900 + 30 − 10 = ₹920 crore. Product taxes and subsidies are added and subtracted only when moving further from basic prices to market prices.',
      'Basic prices = factor cost + production taxes − production subsidies.', 35, 'moderate', '900+30-10'),
    Q('c3', 'Residents of a country earn ₹300 crore of factor income abroad, and non-residents earn ₹350 crore of factor income inside the country. Net factor income from abroad (in ₹ crore) is:',
      ['-50', '50', '650', '-650'],
      'NFIA = factor income earned by residents from abroad − factor income earned by non-residents in the domestic territory = 300 − 350 = −₹50 crore. Since NFIA is negative, the country\'s GNP is smaller than its GDP by ₹50 crore.',
      'NFIA = earned abroad by residents − earned here by non-residents.', 35, 'moderate', '300-350'),
    Q('c5', 'National income of a country is ₹2,00,000 crore and its population is 50 crore. Per capita income is:',
      ['₹4,000', '₹40,000', '₹400', '₹10,000'],
      'Per capita income = national income ÷ population = ₹2,00,000 crore ÷ 50 crore = ₹4,000 per person. The "crore" cancels out, so do not add zeros. Per capita income is a rough indicator of the average standard of living.',
      'Per capita = NI ÷ population (crore cancels crore).', 30, 'core', '200000/50'),
    Q('c1', 'In India, official national income estimates are compiled by the:',
      ['National Statistical Office (NSO) under the Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation', 'Reserve Bank of India', 'NITI Aayog', 'Finance Commission'],
      'National accounts statistics in India are prepared by the National Statistical Office, which functions under the Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation (MoSPI). The RBI, NITI Aayog and the Finance Commission use these estimates, but they do not compile the official national income figures.',
      'National income data = NSO (MoSPI).', 20),
    Q('c11', 'The value of household work done by home-makers for their own families is not included in national income. As a result, national income:',
      ['Understates the true value of production and welfare', 'Overstates economic welfare', 'Becomes negative', 'Includes double counting'],
      'Non-market activities such as cooking, cleaning and caring by home-makers produce real value but have no market price, so they are left out of national income. This is one limitation of national income as a welfare measure: it understates the true production of the economy.',
      'Unpaid home work left out -> NI understates welfare.', 25),
]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Ch 5 Unit 2 Keynesian theory
BANK['fnd-p4-ch05-u02'] = [
    Q('c6', 'If the marginal propensity to consume is 0.8, the investment multiplier is:',
      ['5', '1.25', '0.2', '8'],
      'With an MPC of 0.8, the MPS is 0.2, and the investment multiplier is the reciprocal of the MPS: 1/0.2 = 5. A rise in investment of ₹1 would raise national income by ₹5 in a simple two-sector model.',
      'k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ MPS.', 25, 'core', '1/(1-0.8)'),
    Q('c6', 'Investment rises by ₹200 crore and the marginal propensity to save is 0.25. The increase in national income is:',
      ['₹800 crore', '₹50 crore', '₹250 crore', '₹1,000 crore'],
      'Multiplier k = 1 ÷ MPS = 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4. Change in income = k × change in investment = 4 × 200 = ₹800 crore. Multiplying by MPS instead of dividing gives the wrong answer of ₹50 crore.',
      'ΔY = ΔI ÷ MPS.', 35, 'core', '200/0.25'),
    Q('c4', 'In a two-sector economy, C = 50 + 0.75Y and investment is ₹100 crore. Equilibrium national income is:',
      ['₹600 crore', '₹200 crore', '₹150 crore', '₹450 crore'],
      'At equilibrium Y = C + I = 50 + 0.75Y + 100, so 0.25Y = 150 and Y = ₹600 crore. Check: C = 50 + 450 = 500, and C + I = 600. The value 150 is autonomous spending, which must be multiplied by 1 ÷ (1 − 0.75) = 4.',
      'Y = (autonomous C + I) ÷ (1 − MPC).', 45, 'moderate', '(50+100)/(1-0.75)'),
    Q('c3', 'At an income of ₹1,000, consumption is ₹850. The average propensity to save is:',
      ['0.15', '0.85', '1.18', '0.5'],
      'Saving = income − consumption = 1,000 − 850 = ₹150. Average propensity to save = S ÷ Y = 150 ÷ 1,000 = 0.15. The average propensity to consume is 0.85, and APC + APS always equals one.',
      'APS = S ÷ Y = 1 − APC.', 30, 'core', '(1000-850)/1000'),
    Q('c3', 'The consumption function is C = 100 + 0.6Y. Saving at an income of ₹500 is:',
      ['₹100', '₹400', '₹200', '-₹100'],
      'The matching saving function is S = Y − C = −100 + 0.4Y. At Y = 500, S = −100 + 200 = ₹100. Equivalently, C = 100 + 300 = 400, so S = 500 − 400 = ₹100. The −100 is dissaving at zero income, not saving at ₹500.',
      'S = −a + (1 − b)Y.', 40, 'moderate', '-100+0.4*500'),
    Q('c7', 'In a three-sector model, C = 40 + 0.8Yd, taxes T = 50 (lump sum), investment I = 60 and government purchases G = 50. Equilibrium income is:',
      ['550', '750', '500', '600'],
      'Disposable income Yd = Y − 50. Y = C + I + G = 40 + 0.8(Y − 50) + 60 + 50 = 110 + 0.8Y, so 0.2Y = 110 and Y = 550. Forgetting to subtract taxes from income gives (40 + 60 + 50) ÷ 0.2 = 750, a common slip.',
      'Use Yd = Y − T inside C, then solve Y = C + I + G.', 60, 'hard', '(40-0.8*50+60+50)/(1-0.8)'),
    Q('c7', 'With an MPC of 0.75, the multiplier for a change in lump-sum taxes is:',
      ['-3', '4', '-4', '3'],
      'The tax multiplier is minus MPC divided by MPS; with MPC 0.75 and MPS 0.25 that is minus three. A tax cut of ₹1 raises disposable income by ₹1, but only ₹0.75 is spent in the first round, so the tax multiplier is smaller (in absolute value) than the spending multiplier of 4, and negative.',
      'Tax multiplier = −b ÷ (1 − b).', 40, 'hard', '-0.75/(1-0.75)'),
    Q('c8', 'In an open economy, MPC is 0.8 and the marginal propensity to import is 0.05. The multiplier is:',
      ['4', '5', '6.67', '3.33'],
      'In the four-sector model, part of each round of spending leaks out as imports. The multiplier = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC + MPM) = 1 ÷ (1 − 0.8 + 0.05) = 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4. Without imports it would be 5; imports reduce the multiplier.',
      'Open economy k = 1 ÷ (MPS + MPM).', 40, 'moderate', '1/(1-0.8+0.05)'),
    Q('c6', 'MPC is 0.9. To raise national income by ₹1,000 crore, investment must rise by:',
      ['₹100 crore', '₹900 crore', '₹1,000 crore', '₹111 crore'],
      'Multiplier k = 1 ÷ (1 − 0.9) = 10. Required change in investment = desired change in income ÷ k = 1,000 ÷ 10 = ₹100 crore. A high MPC means a large multiplier, so a small injection of investment produces a large increase in income.',
      'Required ΔI = target ΔY ÷ k.', 35, 'moderate', '1000/(1/(1-0.9))'),
    Q('c5', 'At full employment, the economy\'s income is ₹1,000 crore, but aggregate demand at that income is ₹1,050 crore. There is:',
      ['An inflationary gap of ₹50 crore', 'A deflationary gap of ₹50 crore', 'An inflationary gap of ₹1,050 crore', 'No gap'],
      'An inflationary gap is the excess of aggregate demand over the output the economy can produce at full employment. Here AD exceeds full-employment output by 1,050 − 1,000 = ₹50 crore. Since output cannot rise further, the excess demand pushes up prices. A deflationary gap would arise if AD fell short.',
      'AD above full-employment output = inflationary gap.', 35, 'moderate'),
    Q('c2', 'In the circular flow of income, which of these is a leakage (withdrawal)?',
      ['Saving by households', 'Investment by firms', 'Government expenditure', 'Exports'],
      'Leakages are parts of income that are not passed on as spending on domestic output: saving, taxes and imports. Injections are additions to spending that do not come from household consumption: investment, government expenditure and exports. Income is in equilibrium when total leakages equal total injections.',
      'Withdrawals: saving, taxes, imports. Additions: investment, government spending, exports.', 25),
    Q('c9', 'Assertion (A): If all households try to save more, total saving in the economy may not increase.  Reason (R): Higher saving reduces consumption and aggregate demand, which lowers income and therefore the ability to save.',
      ar(0),
      'Both are true and R explains A. This is Keynes\'s paradox of thrift: when everyone tries to save more, spending falls, firms cut output and income declines through the multiplier. With lower income, total saving may end up unchanged or even lower. What is prudent for one family can be harmful for the whole economy.',
      'Paradox of thrift — R gives the mechanism, so R explains A.', 45, 'moderate'),
    Q('c4', 'Two-sector income is in equilibrium when:',
      ['Planned saving equals planned investment', 'Consumption equals saving', 'Saving is zero', 'Investment equals consumption'],
      'In a two-sector economy, income is in equilibrium when aggregate demand (C + I) equals output, which is the same as saying that planned saving equals planned investment. If planned investment exceeds planned saving, demand exceeds output and income rises; if it falls short, income falls.',
      'Two-sector equilibrium: Y = C + I  <=>  S = I.', 25),
]

