# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Ch 7 Unit 1 Fiscal functions
BANK['fnd-p4-ch07-u01'] = [
    Q('c4', 'A government levies progressive income tax and uses the revenue for food subsidies to poor households. This mainly serves Musgrave\'s:',
      ['Distribution (redistribution) function', 'Allocation function', 'Stabilisation function', 'Regulatory function'],
      'Musgrave divided the fiscal role of government into allocation, distribution and stabilisation. Taxing the rich at higher rates and transferring benefits to the poor changes the distribution of income and wealth, so it is the distribution function. Allocation concerns which goods are provided, and stabilisation concerns inflation and unemployment.',
      'Taking from rich, giving to poor = distribution.', 30),
    Q('c3', 'Provision of national defence by the government falls under the:',
      ['Allocation function', 'Distribution function', 'Stabilisation function', 'Monetary function'],
      'National defence is a public good that the market would not provide adequately, because people cannot be excluded from its benefit. Deciding to provide such goods and directing resources towards them is the allocation function of government. Distribution relates to fairness of income, stabilisation to macroeconomic stability.',
      'Providing public goods = allocation.', 25),
    Q('c5', 'Raising taxes and cutting government spending to control high inflation is an example of the:',
      ['Stabilisation function', 'Allocation function', 'Distribution function', 'Regulatory function'],
      'The stabilisation function uses fiscal policy to keep the economy close to full employment with stable prices. During high inflation, the government reduces aggregate demand by raising taxes or cutting spending; in a recession it does the opposite. This is a macroeconomic function.',
      'Inflation/unemployment control = stabilisation.', 25),
    Q('c7', 'The Finance Commission of India is constituted under:',
      ['Article 280, normally every five years', 'Article 112, every year', 'Article 279A, every two years', 'Article 266, every ten years'],
      'Article 280 of the Constitution requires the President to constitute a Finance Commission every five years, or earlier if needed. Its main task is to recommend how the net proceeds of shareable Central taxes are divided between the Centre and the States, and among the States. Article 279A deals with the GST Council.',
      'Finance Commission = Article 280 = 5 years.', 25),
    Q('c6', 'Under the Constitution, tax on agricultural income can be levied by:',
      ['State governments', 'The Central Government', 'Local panchayats only', 'The GST Council'],
      'The power to tax agricultural income is given to the States through the State List. The Centre levies income tax on non-agricultural income. This is an example of how the Constitution divides tax powers between the two levels of government in India\'s fiscal federalism.',
      'Agricultural income tax = States.', 25),
    Q('c8', 'The GST Council, which recommends GST rates and rules, is chaired by:',
      ['The Union Finance Minister', 'The Prime Minister', 'The Governor of the RBI', 'The Chairman of the Finance Commission'],
      'The GST Council, set up under Article 279A, consists of the Union Finance Minister as Chairperson, the Union Minister of State for Finance, and the Finance or Taxation Ministers of the States. It is a joint forum of the Centre and the States to recommend GST rates, exemptions and rules.',
      'GST Council chair = Union Finance Minister.', 25),
    Q('c6', 'In India, the States\' expenditure responsibilities are larger than their own revenue powers, while the Centre has the more buoyant taxes. This mismatch is called:',
      ['Vertical fiscal imbalance', 'Horizontal fiscal imbalance', 'Revenue surplus', 'Crowding out'],
      'Vertical fiscal imbalance is the gap between the revenue-raising powers and the spending responsibilities of different levels of government. Horizontal imbalance refers to differences among States themselves. Finance Commission transfers and grants help correct both kinds of imbalance.',
      'Centre vs States mismatch = vertical; State vs State = horizontal.', 30, 'moderate'),
]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Ch 7 Unit 2 Market failure
BANK['fnd-p4-ch07-u02'] = [
    Q('c3', 'A chemical factory discharges waste into a river and harms fishermen downstream without compensating them. This is an example of a:',
      ['Negative production externality', 'Positive consumption externality', 'Public good', 'Merit good'],
      'An externality is a cost or benefit falling on third parties who are not part of the transaction. Here the factory\'s production imposes costs on fishermen, so it is a negative production externality. The social cost of the factory\'s output exceeds its private cost, so the market overproduces it.',
      'Harm to outsiders from production = negative production externality.', 30),
    Q('c3', 'When a person gets vaccinated, others around him are also less likely to catch the disease. This is a:',
      ['Positive externality', 'Negative externality', 'Case of moral hazard', 'Case of adverse selection'],
      'Vaccination benefits not only the person vaccinated but also others, who face a lower risk of infection. This spill-over benefit is a positive externality. Because private buyers ignore the benefit to others, the market tends to under-supply such goods, which justifies subsidies or free provision.',
      'Benefit to outsiders = positive externality.', 25),
    Q('c4', 'A city\'s festival fireworks can be watched by everyone in the area, nobody can be stopped from watching, and one more viewer does not reduce anyone else\'s enjoyment. Economically, the display is:',
      ['A pure public good', 'A private good', 'A common access resource', 'A demerit good'],
      'The display is non-excludable, because viewers cannot be prevented from watching, and non-rival, because one more viewer does not reduce what others get. Goods with both features are pure public goods, which markets under-provide because of free riders. A common access resource is rival, and a private good is both rival and excludable.',
      'Non-rival + non-excludable = public good.', 30),
    Q('c4', 'Fishing grounds in the open sea are used by everyone and gradually get over-fished. This illustrates:',
      ['The tragedy of the commons for a common access resource', 'A pure public good', 'A merit good', 'Moral hazard'],
      'Open-sea fisheries are non-excludable (anyone can fish) but rival (fish caught by one are not available to others). Each user ignores the effect of his catch on others, so the resource is over-used and depleted — the tragedy of the commons. This is a common access resource, not a pure public good.',
      'Rival + non-excludable = common resource -> over-use.', 30, 'moderate'),
    Q('c5', 'After buying comprehensive vehicle insurance, a driver starts parking carelessly in unsafe places. This is an example of:',
      ['Moral hazard', 'Adverse selection', 'Free riding on a public good', 'A positive externality'],
      'Moral hazard arises after a contract is made, when one party changes behaviour because it no longer bears the full cost of its actions. Being insured, the driver takes less care. Adverse selection arises before the contract, when the party with hidden information is more likely to enter it.',
      'Behaviour changes AFTER the deal = moral hazard; hidden info BEFORE = adverse selection.', 30),
    Q('c5', 'In a used-car market, buyers cannot tell good cars from bad ones, so good cars are withdrawn and mostly poor-quality cars are left for sale. This is:',
      ['Adverse selection due to asymmetric information', 'Moral hazard', 'A negative externality', 'Price discrimination'],
      'When sellers know more about quality than buyers, buyers offer only an average price. Owners of good cars find this too low and withdraw, so the market is left mostly with poor-quality cars ("lemons"). This is adverse selection arising from asymmetric information before the transaction.',
      'Hidden quality before the sale = adverse selection.', 30),
    Q('c7', 'A tax levied on a polluting firm equal to the external cost of its pollution is called a:',
      ['Pigouvian tax', 'Progressive tax', 'Customs duty', 'Lump-sum tax'],
      'A Pigouvian tax, named after A. C. Pigou, is set equal to the marginal external cost of an activity. It makes the polluter bear the full social cost, raising the price of the product and reducing output towards the socially efficient level.',
      'Tax = external cost -> Pigouvian.', 25),
    Q('c8', 'Education and health care are often subsidised by governments because they are:',
      ['Merit goods that people tend to under-consume', 'Demerit goods', 'Pure public goods in every case', 'Goods with no external benefits'],
      'Merit goods are goods whose benefits to the individual and to society are greater than people realise, so they would be under-consumed if left to the market. Governments therefore subsidise or provide them free. Demerit goods, such as tobacco, are over-consumed and are taxed or restricted.',
      'Under-consumed but good for society = merit good -> subsidise.', 25),
    Q('c9', 'When the government fixes a maximum price for a good below its equilibrium price, the likely result is:',
      ['A shortage, and possibly black markets', 'A surplus of the good', 'No effect on the market', 'A rise in the market price above equilibrium'],
      'A price ceiling below equilibrium makes the good cheaper, so quantity demanded rises while quantity supplied falls, creating a shortage. Rationing, queues and black markets often follow. A price floor above equilibrium, such as a minimum support price, creates a surplus instead.',
      'Ceiling below equilibrium = shortage; floor above = surplus.', 30),
]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Ch 7 Unit 3 Budget making
BANK['fnd-p4-ch07-u03'] = [
    Q('c6', 'Total expenditure of the government is ₹4,500 crore, revenue receipts are ₹3,000 crore and non-debt capital receipts are ₹200 crore. The fiscal deficit is:',
      ['₹1,300 crore', '₹1,500 crore', '₹1,700 crore', '₹1,100 crore'],
      'Fiscal deficit = total expenditure − (revenue receipts + non-debt capital receipts) = 4,500 − (3,000 + 200) = ₹1,300 crore. It measures how much the government must borrow. Forgetting the non-debt capital receipts, such as recoveries of loans and disinvestment, gives ₹1,500 crore.',
      'FD = total expenditure − revenue receipts − non-debt capital receipts.', 40, 'core', '4500-(3000+200)'),
    Q('c6', 'The fiscal deficit is ₹1,300 crore and interest payments are ₹500 crore. The primary deficit is:',
      ['₹800 crore', '₹1,800 crore', '₹500 crore', '₹1,300 crore'],
      'Primary deficit = fiscal deficit − interest payments = 1,300 − 500 = ₹800 crore. It shows how much of the current year\'s borrowing is needed for purposes other than paying interest on past debt. A zero primary deficit means borrowing only covers interest.',
      'PD = FD − interest.', 30, 'core', '1300-500'),
    Q('c6', 'Revenue expenditure is ₹3,600 crore, revenue receipts ₹3,000 crore, and grants for creation of capital assets ₹150 crore. The effective revenue deficit is:',
      ['₹450 crore', '₹600 crore', '₹750 crore', '₹150 crore'],
      'Revenue deficit = 3,600 − 3,000 = ₹600 crore. Effective revenue deficit = revenue deficit − grants for creation of capital assets = 600 − 150 = ₹450 crore. Such grants are shown as revenue spending but actually create assets, so they are excluded.',
      'ERD = RD − grants for capital assets.', 40, 'moderate', '(3600-3000)-150'),
    Q('c4', 'Pick the capital receipt of the government from the list below:',
      ['Proceeds from disinvestment of shares in public sector enterprises', 'GST collections', 'Dividends received from public sector enterprises', 'Interest received on loans given'],
      'Capital receipts either create a liability (such as borrowings) or reduce the government\'s assets (such as disinvestment and recovery of loans). Selling shares in public enterprises reduces assets, so it is a capital receipt. Taxes, dividends and interest are revenue receipts.',
      'Capital receipt = creates liability or reduces assets.', 30),
    Q('c4', 'Dividends and profits received by the government from public sector enterprises are:',
      ['Non-tax revenue receipts', 'Tax revenue receipts', 'Capital receipts', 'Borrowings'],
      'Revenue receipts are divided into tax revenue and non-tax revenue. Dividends and profits from public enterprises, interest receipts, fees and fines are non-tax revenue, because they are not compulsory taxes and they neither create liabilities nor reduce assets.',
      'Dividends/interest/fees = non-tax revenue.', 25),
    Q('c3', 'The Union Budget is presented to Parliament as the "Annual Financial Statement" under:',
      ['Article 112', 'Article 280', 'Article 266', 'Article 32'],
      'Under Article 112, the President must have the Union government\'s estimated income and spending for the coming financial year placed before both Houses of Parliament. This statement is the Annual Financial Statement, popularly called the Budget.',
      'Budget = Annual Financial Statement = Article 112.', 25),
    Q('c8', 'All revenues received by the Government of India, loans raised by it and repayments of loans given by it form the:',
      ['Consolidated Fund of India', 'Contingency Fund of India', 'Public Account of India', 'National Investment Fund'],
      'Under Article 266(1), revenues, loans raised and loan repayments received by the Union form the Consolidated Fund of India, and no money can be withdrawn from it without Parliament\'s authorisation. The Contingency Fund (Article 267) is for urgent unforeseen spending, and the Public Account holds money the government holds as a banker or trustee.',
      'Main account of government money = Consolidated Fund.', 30, 'moderate'),
    Q('c3', 'A "vote on account" allows the government to:',
      ['Withdraw money for a part of the year until the full budget is passed', 'Borrow from the IMF', 'Change tax rates without Parliament', 'Spend from the Public Account without limit'],
      'Passing the full budget takes time. A vote on account lets Parliament grant an advance to the government for expenditure for part of the financial year, so that essential spending can continue until the Appropriation Bill for the whole year is passed. It is common in election years.',
      'Vote on account = interim spending permission.', 25),
]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Ch 7 Unit 4 Fiscal policy
BANK['fnd-p4-ch07-u04'] = [
    Q('c3', 'During a recession with high unemployment, an appropriate fiscal policy is to:',
      ['Increase government spending and/or cut taxes', 'Cut government spending and raise taxes', 'Raise the repo rate', 'Increase the cash reserve ratio'],
      'An expansionary fiscal policy raises aggregate demand by increasing government spending or reducing taxes, which helps output and employment recover. Cutting spending and raising taxes would deepen the recession. Changing the repo rate and CRR are monetary policy tools of the RBI, not fiscal measures.',
      'Recession -> expansionary fiscal policy (G up, T down).', 25),
    Q('c5', 'Progressive income taxes and unemployment benefits reduce fluctuations without any fresh government decision. They are called:',
      ['Automatic (built-in) stabilisers', 'Discretionary fiscal measures', 'Monetary policy tools', 'Supply shocks'],
      'Automatic stabilisers work on their own: in a boom, incomes rise and tax collections rise faster under progressive taxes, restraining demand; in a recession, tax collections fall and benefit payments rise, supporting demand. Discretionary measures, by contrast, need deliberate new decisions by the government.',
      'Works without a new decision = automatic stabiliser.', 30),
    Q('c8', 'Heavy government borrowing pushes up interest rates and reduces private investment. This is called:',
      ['Crowding out', 'The multiplier effect', 'Deficit financing by the central bank', 'Fiscal drag'],
      'When the government borrows heavily from the market, it competes with private borrowers for limited savings, pushing interest rates up. Some private investment becomes unprofitable and is dropped, so government spending partly displaces private spending. This is crowding out, a limitation of fiscal policy.',
      'Govt borrowing squeezes private investment = crowding out.', 25),
    Q('c8', 'Which of these is a limitation of fiscal policy?',
      ['Time lags in recognising the problem, deciding and implementing measures', 'It can never affect aggregate demand', 'It is controlled by the central bank', 'It has no effect on income distribution'],
      'Fiscal policy suffers from recognition, decision and implementation lags, so measures may take effect after conditions have changed. Political pressures and crowding out are other limitations. It does affect aggregate demand and income distribution, and it is controlled by the government, not the central bank.',
      'Fiscal policy weakness = lags + politics + crowding out.', 30),
    Q('c3', 'To control demand-pull inflation, the government should follow a:',
      ['Contractionary fiscal policy — lower spending and/or higher taxes', 'Expansionary fiscal policy', 'Policy of higher subsidies', 'Policy of tax cuts'],
      'Demand-pull inflation arises when aggregate demand exceeds what the economy can produce. A contractionary fiscal policy reduces aggregate demand by cutting government spending or raising taxes, easing pressure on prices. Higher subsidies and tax cuts would add to demand and worsen inflation.',
      'Inflation -> contractionary fiscal policy.', 25),
    Q('c7', 'Which fiscal measure directly helps to reduce inequality of incomes?',
      ['Progressive taxation combined with welfare spending on the poor', 'A uniform lump-sum tax on everyone', 'Higher taxes on essential goods', 'Cutting spending on public education'],
      'Progressive taxation takes a larger share of higher incomes, and the revenue can finance welfare schemes, education and health for the poor. Together they narrow income gaps. A uniform lump-sum tax and higher taxes on essentials fall more heavily on the poor, and cutting education spending would widen inequality.',
      'Equity tools = progressive tax + pro-poor spending.', 30),
    Q('c6', 'A government finances part of its deficit by borrowing from the central bank, which creates new money. The main risk is:',
      ['Inflation, because money supply increases', 'Deflation', 'A fall in aggregate demand', 'Crowding out through higher interest rates only'],
      'When the central bank lends to the government by creating new money, the money supply increases. If output does not rise as fast, more money chases the same goods and prices rise. That is why monetised deficit financing is regarded as inflationary and is now restricted in India.',
      'Printing money to fund deficits -> inflation risk.', 30, 'moderate'),
]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Ch 8 Unit 1 Demand for money
BANK['fnd-p4-ch08-u01'] = [
    Q('c3', 'In Fisher\'s equation MV = PT, the money supply is ₹500 crore, velocity is 4 and the volume of transactions is 1,000 crore units. The price level is:',
      ['2', '0.5', '8', '20'],
      'From MV = PT, P = MV ÷ T = (500 × 4) ÷ 1,000 = 2,000 ÷ 1,000 = 2. Fisher\'s quantity theory assumes V and T are constant in the short run, so the price level changes in proportion to the money supply.',
      'P = MV ÷ T.', 35, 'core', '500*4/1000'),
    Q('c3', 'According to Fisher\'s quantity theory, if the money supply doubles while V and T are constant, the price level will:',
      ['Double', 'Halve', 'Remain unchanged', 'Rise by 25%'],
      'With V and T held constant, MV = PT means that P changes in the same proportion as M. If M doubles, P doubles and the value of money is halved. This proportional relationship is the core of the classical quantity theory of money.',
      'V, T fixed -> P moves one-for-one with M.', 25),
    Q('c4', 'In the Cambridge equation Md = kPY, k = 0.25 and nominal income PY = ₹4,000 crore. The demand for money is:',
      ['₹1,000 crore', '₹16,000 crore', '₹4,000 crore', '₹250 crore'],
      'Md = k × PY = 0.25 × 4,000 = ₹1,000 crore. Here k is the proportion of nominal income people wish to hold as cash balances; it is the reciprocal of the income velocity of money (velocity = 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4). Dividing by k instead of multiplying gives ₹16,000 crore.',
      'Md = k × PY; k = 1 ÷ V.', 30, 'core', '0.25*4000'),
    Q('c3', 'Nominal GDP is ₹3,000 lakh crore and the money supply is ₹600 lakh crore. The income velocity of money is:',
      ['5', '0.2', '2,400', '3.6'],
      'Income velocity of money = nominal income ÷ money supply = 3,000 ÷ 600 = 5. On average, each rupee changes hands five times in the year in buying final goods and services. In the Cambridge version, k = 1 ÷ V = 0.2.',
      'V = PY ÷ M.', 30, 'core', '3000/600'),
    Q('c7', 'Under the Baumol inventory approach, the optimal size of each conversion of bonds into cash is √(2bY/r). If the brokerage cost per conversion b = ₹10, income Y = ₹2,000 and the interest rate r = 0.04, the optimal size of each conversion is:',
      ['₹1,000', '₹100', '₹10,000', '₹500'],
      'Optimal conversion = √(2 × 10 × 2,000 ÷ 0.04) = √(40,000 ÷ 0.04) = √10,00,000 = ₹1,000, so the average cash held would be half of this. The formula shows that money demand rises with income and brokerage cost but falls as the interest rate rises, though less than proportionately (the square-root rule).',
      'Baumol: √(2bY/r).', 50, 'hard', '(2*10*2000/0.04)**0.5'),
    Q('c5', 'In Keynes\'s theory, how does the speculative demand for money move with the interest rate?',
      ['Inversely related to the rate of interest', 'Directly related to the rate of interest', 'Independent of the rate of interest', 'Determined only by income'],
      'People hold money for speculation when they expect bond prices to fall (interest rates to rise). When the interest rate is high, people expect it to fall and bond prices to rise, so they prefer bonds and hold little speculative money. When the rate is low, they hold more money. Hence an inverse relationship.',
      'Speculative Md: high interest -> hold bonds, low money.', 30),
    Q('c6', 'In a liquidity trap:',
      ['Monetary policy becomes ineffective, as extra money is simply held at a very low interest rate', 'Interest rates are very high', 'People refuse to hold any money', 'The demand for money is perfectly inelastic'],
      'At a very low interest rate, everyone expects rates to rise and bond prices to fall, so people are willing to hold any extra money instead of buying bonds. The demand for money becomes perfectly elastic, and increases in the money supply cannot lower the interest rate further. Monetary policy loses its power.',
      'Liquidity trap = very low rate + perfectly elastic Md + monetary policy useless.', 35, 'moderate'),
    Q('c1', 'A loan taken today is to be repaid in money after two years. Here money is serving as a:',
      ['Standard of deferred payments', 'Medium of exchange', 'Unit of account only', 'Store of value only'],
      'Money serves as a standard of deferred payments when future payments, such as loan repayments, salaries due or instalments, are expressed in money. Its other functions are medium of exchange, unit of account (measure of value) and store of value.',
      'Future payment in money = standard of deferred payments.', 25),
    Q('c8', 'Milton Friedman treated the demand for money as depending mainly on:',
      ['Permanent income and the returns on other assets', 'Only the transactions motive', 'Only the speculative motive', 'Sunspots'],
      'In his restatement of the quantity theory, Friedman treated money as one of several assets in which wealth can be held. The demand for money depends on permanent income (a measure of wealth), the expected returns on bonds, equities and goods, and inflation. He found money demand to be fairly stable.',
      'Friedman = money as an asset; permanent income.', 30, 'moderate'),
]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Ch 8 Unit 2 Money supply
